<article class="env-example" id="0185">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0185">Example <span data-tag="0185">4.3.5.10</span>.</a> Let $Y$ be a topological space equipped with a base point $y$. Let $P = \{  p: [0,1] \rightarrow Y \} $ denote the collection of all continuous functions from the unit interval $[0,1]$ to $Y$, and let $P_{y} = \{  p \in P: p(1) = y \} $ denote the subset of $P$ consisting of those continuous paths which end at the point $y$. We regard $P$ as a topological space by equipping it with the compact-open topology, so the singular simplicial set $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(P)$ can be identified with $\operatorname{Fun}( \Delta ^1, \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(Y) )$ (see Warning <a data-tag="00KL" href="/tag/00KL">2.4.2.18</a>). Identifying $y$ with a vertex of the singular simplicial set $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(Y)$, Example <a data-tag="0184" href="/tag/0184">4.3.5.9</a> supplies an isomorphism of simplicial sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(Y)_{/y} \simeq \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(P) \times _{ \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(Y) } \{ y\}  = \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(P_ y). \]
</div>
<p> In particular, since the topological space $P_ y$ is contractible, the simplicial set $\operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(Y)_{/y}$ is a contractible Kan complex (this is a special case of a general phenomenon: see Corollary <a data-tag="018Y" href="/tag/018Y">4.3.7.14</a>). </p>
</article>
